---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { BENCH, SITE } from '../data/site';

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'TechArticle',
    headline: 'HailBrowser browser-use benchmark: 26 tasks at a 4.2s average',
    description:
      'Ultrafast passed 26 of 26 browser-use tasks at a 4.2s average across three full runs, against 7.8s in Chat mode. Per-task times, live runs, method and caveats.',
    datePublished: '2026-10-30',
    dateModified: '2026-10-31',
    author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Shyamnath Sankar', url: 'https://shyamnathsankar.genrecai.com/' },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'GenrecAI', url: 'https://genrecai.com' },
    mainEntityOfPage: `${SITE.url}/benchmarks`,
    about: { '@type': 'SoftwareApplication', name: 'HailBrowser', applicationCategory: 'BrowserApplication' },
    citation: BENCH.script,
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: `${SITE.url}/` },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Benchmarks', item: `${SITE.url}/benchmarks` },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Fastest Browser Use Benchmark: 26/26 at 4.2s"
  description="The fastest browser use ever made: 26 of 26 tasks at 4.2s over three runs, and 16 of 16 live sites. Every per-task time, the method and the script."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <div class="frame">
    <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span aria-current="page">Benchmarks</span>
    </nav>

    <header class="page-hero">
      <div class="page-hero__meta">
        <span class="pill pill--ink">Latest full run</span>
        <span class="pill">Ultrafast · Mercury Decide</span>
      </div>
      <h1>26 tasks.<br />4.2 seconds each.</h1>
      <p>
        Ultrafast passed every task in our browser-use harness, three full runs in a row, at a
        4.2–4.3 second average. That makes it the fastest browser use ever made. The same task set takes
        7.8 seconds in Chat mode. Below is every task, every time, and the method, so you can rerun it
        yourself.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="section section--flush">
      <div class="stats">
        <div class="stat">
          <b>26/26</b>
          <span>Tasks passed</span>
          <em>Three separate full runs. No partial credit.</em>
        </div>
        <div class="stat">
          <b>4.2s</b>
          <span>Average per task</span>
          <em>4.2–4.3s across the three runs.</em>
        </div>
        <div class="stat">
          <b>0</b>
          <span>False “done” claims</span>
          <em>A claim is checked before a run reports success.</em>
        </div>
        <div class="stat">
          <b>69</b>
          <span>Browser actions</span>
          <em>127 decision calls, 4 text-writing calls.</em>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div>
          <p class="label">Fast against Chat</p>
          <h2 class="display-m">One number for the whole loop.</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="body-muted">
          Fast and Chat run the same browser, the same tools and the same task pages. The difference is
          how a decision is made: one operation-and-element choice per step, instead of a full
          tool-calling conversation.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="data">
        <table>
          <caption>Headline numbers, latest full run</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Measure</th><th scope="col">Fast (ultrafast)</th><th scope="col">Chat</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {BENCH.compare.map((row) => (
              <tr><td>{row.metric}</td><td>{row.fast}</td><td>{row.chat}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="note">
        One full Fast run of 26 tasks: {BENCH.actions} browser actions, {BENCH.decisions} decision calls
        and {BENCH.textCalls} text-writing calls. Chat was measured on the original 17-task set, so read
        the averages next to that difference.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div>
          <p class="label">How it got there</p>
          <h2 class="display-m">Four upgrades, one loop.</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="body-muted">
          The score did not start at 26/26. It started at 10/17 with one false completion. Each line is a
          change to the loop, not to the task set.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="data">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Change</th><th scope="col">Score</th><th scope="col">Note</th><th scope="col">Avg time</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {BENCH.progress.map((row) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{row.stage}</td>
                <td class="num">{row.score}</td>
                <td>{row.note}</td>
                <td class="num">{row.avg}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div>
          <p class="label">Every task</p>
          <h2 class="display-m">The full 26, in order.</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="body-muted">
          Times include the model calls, the page reads and the action. A task with 0 actions is a
          question answered from the page without changing anything.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="data">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Task</th><th scope="col">Actions</th><th scope="col">Time</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {BENCH.tasks.map((row) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{row.task}</td>
                <td class="num">{row.actions}</td>
                <td class="num">{row.time}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="real-sites">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div>
          <p class="label">Real sites</p>
          <h2 class="display-m">16 live sites. 16 passed.</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="body-muted">
          The same Fast loop on the open web: Wikipedia, GitHub, YouTube, MDN, Hacker News and four
          form-test sites, run through the MCP server with run_task. {BENCH.real.passed} of
          {BENCH.real.total} passed at an {BENCH.real.avg} average. Fast finished
          {BENCH.real.fastAlone} on its own; on the other {BENCH.real.takeovers} the chat
          model took over where Fast got stuck, without redoing the finished steps.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="data">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Site</th><th scope="col">Goal</th><th scope="col">Result</th><th scope="col">Actions</th><th scope="col">Time</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {BENCH.real.rows.map((row) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{row.site}</td>
                <td>“{row.goal}”</td>
                <td><span class="pass">{row.result}</span></td>
                <td class="num">{row.actions}</td>
                <td class="num">{row.time}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="note">
        Script: <a class="link" href={BENCH.real.script}>scripts/bench-real.mjs</a>. Live sites change and
        sometimes show bot checks, so expect some run-to-run variation.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div>
          <p class="label">Live runs</p>
          <h2 class="display-m">Real sites, your Chrome.</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="body-muted">
          The harness is not the whole story, so four goals were run on the live web in a normal Chrome
          profile. Four runs is a demonstration, not a statistic.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="data">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Goal</th><th scope="col">Result</th><th scope="col">Time</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {BENCH.live.map((row) => (
              <tr>
                <td>“{row.goal}”</td>
                <td><span class="pass">{row.result}</span></td>
                <td class="num">{row.time}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="split split--wide-right">
        <div class="stack">
          <p class="label">Method</p>
          <h2 class="display-m">Reproducible, not a slide.</h2>
          <p class="body-muted">
            The harness ships with the extension. It serves 26 local pages, drives each goal through
            <code>run_task</code> in ultrafast mode, and scores the page state or the answer.
          </p>
          <div class="bench-actions">
            <a class="btn" href={BENCH.script} rel="noopener" target="_blank">Read the harness <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="prose">
          <h3>What is measured</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Task pages.</strong> 26 local pages with forms, dialogs, dropdowns, hover menus, pagination, tabs, cookie walls and a scrollable panel.</li>
            <li><strong>Scoring.</strong> A task passes only if the page ends in the expected state, or the answer matches the page. A model saying “done” is not a pass.</li>
            <li><strong>Model.</strong> Mercury Decide (Inception) for Fast, Mercury 2 for the Chat comparison. Both calls and page reads are inside the measured time.</li>
            <li><strong>Runs.</strong> Three complete Fast runs of all 26 tasks. Times shown are from the latest complete run.</li>
            <li><strong>Machine.</strong> One desktop machine, Chrome stable, ordinary network. No GPU and no special hosting.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3>Run it yourself</h3>
          <pre class="mono">OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-... \
DECISION_MODEL=inception/mercury-decide \
node scripts/bench-fast.mjs</pre>
          <p>
            Set <code>BENCH_MODE=normal</code> to run the same tasks in Chat mode. The repository keeps
            the harness at 26/26 for Fast-mode changes, so a regression shows up as a failed task, not a
            slower average.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section section--tight">
      <div class="callout-lime">
        <h3>Why we call it the fastest browser use ever made</h3>
        <p>
          Because that is the claim the numbers support. 4.2 seconds per task, 26 of 26, no false
          completions, and a harness anyone can rerun. We have not audited every browser agent ever
          shipped, and we are not going to pretend otherwise. If you have a reproducible run that beats
          this, send it and we will link it here.
        </p>
        <div class="hero__actions">
          <a class="btn btn--sm" href="/browser-use">What browser use is <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
          <a class="btn btn--sm" href="/docs/modes#fast">How Fast works <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>What this benchmark is not</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>It is not a third-party audit. It is our harness, run on our machine, published in full.</li>
          <li>The 26 pages are local and simpler than the live web: no bot walls, no A/B tests, no slow third-party scripts.</li>
          <li>Four live runs show the loop works outside the harness. They are not a sample size.</li>
          <li>Provider latency moves the average. A slower model produces slower runs with the same task score.</li>
          <li>“Fastest” here means fastest measured loop across the open-source browser agents we could test. It is not a claim about every proprietary system ever shipped.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section section--tight">
      <nav class="more-links" aria-label="Related">
        <a href="/browser-use">
          <span class="label">Category</span>
          <b>Browser use</b>
          <em>What the term means, and how this agent does it.</em>
        </a>
        <a href="/comet-alternative">
          <span class="label">Compare</span>
          <b>Open-source Comet</b>
          <em>A Comet-style agent inside Chrome.</em>
        </a>
        <a href="/docs/modes#fast">
          <span class="label">Mechanics</span>
          <b>Fast mode</b>
          <em>One decision per step, explained.</em>
        </a>
        <a href="/use-cases/coding-agents">
          <span class="label">Agents</span>
          <b>Claude Code &amp; MCP</b>
          <em>The same loop, driven by your agent.</em>
        </a>
      </nav>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
